<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      THE platform_db RLS POSTURE — A DECISION, NOT AN OMISSION
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      Measured live 2026-08-12 against the running restaurantos-postgres (PostgreSQL 18.4):

          relname            | rls | forced | policies
          impersonation_log  |  f  |   f    |    0
          tenant_features    |  f  |   f    |    0
          usage_records      |  f  |   f    |    0
          event_outbox       |  f  |   f    |    0

      Three of those carry a tenant_id column, and every other tenant-scoped database in this
      product FORCEs RLS on exactly that column. platform_db does not, on any table, anywhere.

      That reads like the audit_events gap that 030-audit-events-rls.xml closed. IT IS NOT THE
      SAME THING, and applying the same fix here breaks the product. What follows is the trace
      that establishes that, the measurement that proves it, and the one thing that IS wrong.

      ── WHY RLS IS THE WRONG CONTROL HERE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

      A tenant_isolation policy answers the question "which tenant is this connection acting for?"
      by reading app.current_tenant_id. The SuperAdmin console has no answer to that question, by
      construction:

        * JwtSigningService.signPlatformToken mints a control-plane token with the platform user's
          id as the subject and NO tenant_id claim at all. PlatformAdminController's own comment
          says so explicitly — the absent tenant claim is what makes a platform principal
          distinguishable from a tenant one.
        * TenantAwareDataSource therefore writes app.current_tenant_id = '' (EMPTY, not unset) on
          every connection checkout the console makes, deliberately, so a pooled connection can
          never inherit a previous borrower's tenant.
        * The fleet-standard predicate maps that empty string to NULL via NULLIF and fails CLOSED.

      So the policy would not scope the console down to one tenant. It would scope it down to
      ZERO. Measured rather than reasoned — the fleet's exact policy text applied to a copy of
      tenant_features in the rls_probe database, tables owned by a NOSUPERUSER role, read by a
      separate NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS runtime role, GUC set to '' as TenantAwareDataSource sets
      it:

          before:  SELECT count(*)  ->  2      (both tenants, which is the console's job)
          after:   SELECT count(*)  ->  0
          write:   ERROR: new row violates row-level security policy for table
                          "tenant_features_probe"

      Against production numbers that is GET /api/v1/platform/tenants/{id}/features returning an
      empty map for all 402 tenant_features rows across 21 tenants, and PATCH .../features/{code}
      raising 42501 on every call. The feature-flag console would go blank and read as "no tenant
      has any feature enabled" — a fail-closed control-plane failure that looks exactly like a
      product decision. GET /tenants (findAll) is cross-tenant by definition and is the console's
      primary screen.

      Cross-tenant read is not a leak here. It is the job. platform-admin-service exists to look
      at every tenant at once.

      ── WHAT ACTUALLY ENFORCES ISOLATION, AND WHETHER IT HOLDS ───────────────────────────────────

      Isolation for platform_db is BY DATABASE and BY ROLE, not by policy. Both halves were
      measured, not assumed:

        1. No tenant service role can read platform_db. CONNECT is PUBLIC on every database in
           this cluster (PostgreSQL's default), so has_database_privilege is a useless probe and
           was not trusted. The table ACLs were read instead, and then the reach was executed:

               platform_db relacl on tenants / impersonation_log:
                   {platform_admin=arwdDxtm/platform_admin, platform_user=arwdDxtm/platform_admin}
                   — no other grantee, no PUBLIC entry

               SET ROLE pos_user;  SELECT count(*) FROM tenants;
                   ERROR:  permission denied for table tenants
               SET ROLE auth_user; SELECT count(*) FROM impersonation_log;
                   ERROR:  permission denied for table impersonation_log

        2. The platform roles cannot reach tenant data either. Counted across all 14 tenant
           databases (audit, auth, crm, file, finance, hr, inventory, kitchen, nlq, notification,
           pos, purchasing, reporting, user):

               grants held by platform_user or platform_admin  ->  0, in every one

        3. There is no cross-database bridge. platform_db has exactly one extension installed —
           plpgsql. No postgres_fdw, no dblink. A connection to platform_db cannot address another
           database's tables at all.

        4. At the API layer PlatformAdminController is @PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('SUPER_ADMIN')")
           at the CLASS level, and the internal twin sits behind a constant-time X-Internal-Service
           secret check. A tenant user's token, however valid, is not a platform principal.

      A per-tenant restriction would have to apply to a role that can reach platform_db AND ought
      to see only one tenant. There is no such role. platform_admin is Liquibase and the table
      owner; platform_user is the console. Both are legitimately cross-tenant. THE ANSWER TO THE
      QUESTION IS THAT NO ROLE REACHING platform_db SHOULD BE TENANT-RESTRICTED.

      ── SO: RLS STAYS OFF, ON PURPOSE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

      Recorded in three places so it cannot be mistaken for an oversight: this header, a
      COMMENT ON TABLE on each of the three tenant_id-bearing tables (visible to any DBA typing
      \d+ in psql, which is where the question actually gets asked), and PlatformDbIsolationIT,
      which asserts the absence as a NEGATIVE CONTROL and says so in its own class javadoc.

      DO NOT "fix" this by enabling RLS. If a future requirement genuinely needs platform_db
      partitioned by tenant, the mechanism is NOT app.current_tenant_id — the console has no
      tenant. It would need a different GUC carrying the operator's permitted tenant SET, and a
      policy written against that. Enabling the fleet-standard policy makes the console read zero
      rows; that is measured above, not predicted.

      event_outbox is noted and deliberately untouched: it is unforced in ALL 15 databases, which
      is a fleet-wide convention and a fleet-wide question, not this table's problem.

      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      WHAT *IS* WRONG: impersonation_log is not an audit trail, it is a table
      ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

      impersonation_log is the accountability record of platform staff assuming tenant users'
      identities — the row an abuse review reads to answer "who went into this restaurant's data,
      when, as whom, and why". Tenant isolation is the wrong worry for it. Immutability is the
      right one, and it had none. Measured live, as platform_user, the role the console's own
      datasource connects as (transaction rolled back, live count still 0):

          INSERT INTO impersonation_log (...) 'PROBE: went looking at a tenant's payroll'
              -> INSERT 0 1
          UPDATE impersonation_log SET reason='routine support', target_user_id=gen_random_uuid()
              -> UPDATE 1          (the operator rewrites what they did and to whom)
          DELETE FROM impersonation_log WHERE reason='routine support'
              -> DELETE 1          (the operator erases the record entirely)
          TRUNCATE impersonation_log
              -> TRUNCATE TABLE    (the operator erases the whole trail in one statement)

          triggers on impersonation_log: 0
          grants to platform_user:       DELETE, INSERT, REFERENCES, SELECT, TRIGGER, TRUNCATE,
                                         UPDATE  — i.e. everything

      The subject of the record could choose its contents. That is not an accountability trail; it
      is a note the accountable party is free to edit. And nothing in the application needs any of
      it: the ONLY write path is ImpersonationService.impersonate(), which calls save() on a fresh
      entity — insert, once, never updated. impact(ImpersonationLogEntity, upstream) returns 4
      direct dependents, all inside this service, none of which mutate. TenantLifecycleService
      .purge() does not delete these rows either; it flips the tenant's status and nothing more.

      Two layers, matching what 010/011 do for audit_events and what trg_je_immutable does for
      POSTED journal entries.

      ── The read half, stated because it is the other half of the finding ────────────────────────

      There is NO read path. ImpersonationLogRepository.findByTenantIdOrderByStartedAtDesc has
      zero callers; no controller exposes it; no frontend route fetches it. The trail is
      write-only from the product's point of view and can only be read with a psql session. That
      is a real gap in the abuse-review story — an accountability record nobody can read is only
      marginally better than one that is not written — but it is a MISSING FEATURE, not a broken
      control, so it is reported rather than built here. Making the rows unforgeable first is the
      right order: a reader added on top of a mutable table would display whatever the last
      operator decided it should say.
    -->

    <changeSet id="040-001-platform-db-rls-posture-comment" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          The decision, in the database itself. A DBA answering "why does platform_db have no RLS
          when everything else does?" types \d+ in psql long before they find this file, and an
          unexplained absence is what gets "fixed" at 2am.
        -->
        <sql>
            COMMENT ON TABLE impersonation_log IS
            'Accountability record of platform staff impersonating tenant users. NO RLS BY DESIGN: platform-admin-service is a cross-tenant SuperAdmin console whose token carries no tenant_id, so app.current_tenant_id is always empty and the fleet tenant_isolation policy would match ZERO rows. Isolation here is by database and role, not by policy — no tenant role holds any grant in platform_db. APPEND-ONLY, enforced by trg_impersonation_log_immutable + trg_impersonation_log_no_truncate. NOTE: the accompanying REVOKE from platform_user is INERT — platform_user inherits platform_admin (pg_auth_members), so has_table_privilege still reports DELETE; the triggers are the only enforcing layer. See 040-platform-db-rls-posture.xml.';
        </sql>
        <sql>
            COMMENT ON TABLE tenant_features IS
            'Per-tenant feature overrides, read and written cross-tenant by the SuperAdmin console. NO RLS BY DESIGN — see 040-platform-db-rls-posture.xml. tenant_id is a plain FK to tenants(id), not a row-security boundary; a tenant_isolation policy here returns 0 of 402 rows to the console and 42501 on every write (measured).';
        </sql>
        <sql>
            COMMENT ON TABLE usage_records IS
            'Per-tenant usage telemetry, aggregated cross-tenant by the SuperAdmin console. NO RLS BY DESIGN — see 040-platform-db-rls-posture.xml. tenant_id is a plain FK, not a row-security boundary.';
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>COMMENT ON TABLE impersonation_log IS NULL;</sql>
            <sql>COMMENT ON TABLE tenant_features IS NULL;</sql>
            <sql>COMMENT ON TABLE usage_records IS NULL;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="040-002-impersonation-log-append-only-grants" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          Layer 1 — least privilege on the runtime role. READ THE WARNING BELOW: ON THIS CLUSTER
          THIS REVOKE IS CURRENTLY INERT. It is kept because it is correct and becomes load-bearing
          the day the ownership topology is fixed; it is NOT a second enforcing layer today, and
          documenting it as one would be exactly the "control that exists and enforces nothing"
          this repository keeps finding.

          ⚠ MEASURED LIVE, AFTER APPLYING THIS CHANGESET TO platform_db:

              information_schema.role_table_grants  ->  INSERT, REFERENCES, SELECT, TRIGGER
              has_table_privilege('platform_user',
                  'impersonation_log', 'DELETE')    ->  TRUE

          The catalogue view a reviewer would check says the privilege is gone. The effective
          privilege is unchanged. The reason:

              SELECT ... FROM pg_auth_members  ->  platform_user INHERITS platform_admin

          deploy/init/02b-ensure-runtime-roles.sql issues GRANT platform_admin TO platform_user
          deliberately, to reconcile a real problem: the tables are owned by platform_admin on
          machines where that role was created by hand, while the service and Liquibase both
          connect as platform_user, so every migration that ALTERs an existing table would fail.
          Membership restores the ALTER privilege without rewriting ownership of a live database.

          The side effect is that platform_user holds every privilege platform_admin holds,
          including ownership of this table — so a REVOKE of a direct grant changes the ACL and
          changes nothing else. And on a CLEAN machine, where 02b is a documented no-op,
          platform_user OWNS the tables outright, which is inert for the same reason. There is no
          deployment shape in which this REVOKE alone protects the row.

          That is precisely why layer 2 below is a TRIGGER and not a grant. A trigger fires for the
          owner, for an inheriting member, and for Liquibase.

          Two consequences worth carrying out of this file, because they outlive this table:

            * Least privilege is not currently enforceable anywhere in platform_db. Any grant-based
              control on any table here has the same hole.
            * If anyone ever does add RLS to platform_db against the decision recorded above, plain
              ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY would be COMPLETELY INERT — PostgreSQL exempts a table's
              owner from its own policies unless FORCE is set, and the runtime role is the owner.
              That is the identical defect that shipped 33 tables with inert RLS across 16 tenants
              in this product.

          Mechanics: guarded on the role existing. In production deploy/init/02-create-roles.sql
          creates platform_user; under Testcontainers the container superuser is the only role
          present, and a REVOKE naming a role that does not exist is an ERROR, not a no-op — it
          would abort Liquibase and fail every IT in this service at context startup.

          When the "end impersonation session" endpoint that ended_at was designed for is finally
          built, the grant it needs is COLUMN-scoped and nothing wider:

              GRANT UPDATE (ended_at) ON impersonation_log TO platform_user;

          Layer 2 already permits exactly that transition and nothing else, so the two agree.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            BEGIN
                IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'platform_user') THEN
                    REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON impersonation_log FROM platform_user;
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <sql>REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON impersonation_log FROM PUBLIC;</sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="false">
                DO $$
                BEGIN
                    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = 'platform_user') THEN
                        GRANT UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON impersonation_log TO platform_user;
                    END IF;
                END $$;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="040-003-impersonation-log-immutability-trigger" author="restaurantos-agent">
        <!--
          Layer 2 — the trigger, which fires for EVERY role including the table owner and the
          migration tool. This is the layer that makes the record survive the people it holds
          accountable; a control that stops only the role already stopped by GRANT is decoration.
          Same reasoning, and the same shape, as audit-service's 011 and finance's trg_je_immutable.

          DELETE is refused outright. UPDATE is refused with EXACTLY ONE carve-out: closing an open
          session, i.e. ended_at going NULL -> NOT NULL with every other column byte-identical. The
          carve-out exists so that the endpoint ended_at was added for can be built WITHOUT deleting
          this control to do it — a guard whose first encounter with a real feature is to be removed
          protects nothing. IS NOT DISTINCT FROM is used for the nullable columns because
          NULL = NULL is NULL, and a plain = there would silently fall through to the RAISE.

          A row-level trigger does not see TRUNCATE at all — TRUNCATE bypasses row triggers
          entirely, which is why the probe above got "TRUNCATE TABLE" back while holding only
          table grants. It needs its own statement-level trigger, below. Leaving it out would
          produce a table that is immutable row by row and erasable in one statement.

          Retention: there is deliberately no deletion path, and this table is not partitioned, so
          it has no archival equivalent of AuditArchivalService's DETACH. Purging rows requires a
          credentialed superuser session that drops these triggers first — visible, deliberate, and
          exactly as awkward as erasing an accountability trail should be. That is the same trade
          audit_events makes.
        -->
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION prevent_impersonation_log_rewrite() RETURNS trigger AS $$
            BEGIN
                IF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                        'IMPERSONATION_LOG_IMMUTABLE: impersonation records cannot be deleted — this is the accountability trail for platform staff acting as tenant users';
                END IF;

                IF OLD.ended_at IS NULL
                   AND NEW.ended_at IS NOT NULL
                   AND NEW.id               =  OLD.id
                   AND NEW.platform_user_id =  OLD.platform_user_id
                   AND NEW.tenant_id        =  OLD.tenant_id
                   AND NEW.target_user_id   =  OLD.target_user_id
                   AND NEW.started_at       =  OLD.started_at
                   AND NEW.expires_at IS NOT DISTINCT FROM OLD.expires_at
                   AND NEW.reason     IS NOT DISTINCT FROM OLD.reason
                THEN
                    RETURN NEW;
                END IF;

                RAISE EXCEPTION
                    'IMPERSONATION_LOG_IMMUTABLE: the only permitted update is closing an open session (ended_at NULL -> NOT NULL, every other column unchanged)';
            END;
            $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
        </sql>

        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION prevent_impersonation_log_truncate() RETURNS trigger AS $$
            BEGIN
                RAISE EXCEPTION
                    'IMPERSONATION_LOG_IMMUTABLE: impersonation_log cannot be truncated — a row-level trigger does not see TRUNCATE, so this statement-level one exists to stop the whole trail being erased in one statement';
            END;
            $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
        </sql>

        <!--
          DROP before CREATE so re-running against a partially-applied database is a no-op rather
          than a duplicate-object error. CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER exists in PostgreSQL 14+ but
          DROP IF EXISTS is what the rest of this repository's changesets use.
        -->
        <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_impersonation_log_immutable ON impersonation_log;</sql>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE TRIGGER trg_impersonation_log_immutable
                BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON impersonation_log
                FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION prevent_impersonation_log_rewrite();
        </sql>

        <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_impersonation_log_no_truncate ON impersonation_log;</sql>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            CREATE TRIGGER trg_impersonation_log_no_truncate
                BEFORE TRUNCATE ON impersonation_log
                FOR EACH STATEMENT EXECUTE FUNCTION prevent_impersonation_log_truncate();
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_impersonation_log_immutable ON impersonation_log;</sql>
            <sql>DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS trg_impersonation_log_no_truncate ON impersonation_log;</sql>
            <sql>DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS prevent_impersonation_log_rewrite();</sql>
            <sql>DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS prevent_impersonation_log_truncate();</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
